[Event "The main lines at a glance"]
[ChapterName "1...c6 and the Classical 4...Bf5"]
[Orientation "black"]

{ [%ts 0:36] Stjepan starts with the basics: the opening principles and the main line, played move by move. You have Black. }
1. e4 c6 { [%ts 0:44] This is the Caro-Kann. Whatever happens next, c6 is there to support a later d5. }
( 1... e5 ) ( 1... c5 ) ( 1... e6 ) ( 1... Nc6 )
2. d4 { [%ts 0:52] White has three moves here, and d4 is by far the most common. }
2... d5 { [%ts 1:09] In every line Black meets d4 with d5, challenging the center. That's the point of c6: instead of e5 or c5, Black prepares d5 and fights for the center one move later. }
( 2... g6 ) ( 2... d6 ) ( 2... a6 ) ( 2... e6 )
3. Nc3
( 3. e5 { [%ts 1:42] White has a couple of other replies after d5, and this is where the lines branch out. The Advance, e5, gets its own day. } )
3... dxe4 { [%ts 1:58] From here Black really has only one move: take on e4. }
( 3... e6 ) ( 3... h6 ) ( 3... a6 )
4. Nxe4
4... Bf5 { [%ts 2:06] The main move, challenging the knight on e4. }
( 4... Nd7 { [%ts 7:51] The Karpov Variation, a very good option: it has its own chapter. Here, find the main move. } )
( 4... Nf6 { [%ts 11:18] The second most popular move, with its own chapters. Here, find the main move. } )
( 4... g6 ) ( 4... h6 ) ( 4... a5 )
5. Ng3
5... Bg6 { [%ts 2:15] Saving the bishop. This is the starting position of the Caro-Kann main line. }
6. h4 { [%ts 2:23] White's idea is to exploit the bishop on g6, which is slightly misplaced and has already used a couple of tempi. h4 threatens h5, trapping it. }
( 6. Bd3 { [%ts 6:37] White doesn't have to start with h4. Nf3, Be3 or Bd3, which challenges the bishop at once, all avoid weakening the kingside and might surprise your opponent. } )
6... h6 { [%ts 2:39] Making room for the bishop. h5 would be inferior: it weakens too many squares and gives the knight on g3 and the queen a clear target on the h-file. }
7. Nf3
7... Nd7 { [%ts 6:54] The most principled continuation. Black develops the knight to d7 first. }
( 7... Nf6 { [%ts 6:54] Stjepan calls ...Nf6 a mistake here because Ne5 gives White a lot of activity, though the engine rates it close to ...Nd7. Play his move: develop the knight to d7 first. } )
( 7... e6 ) ( 7... Bh7 )
8. h5 { [%ts 2:53] h5 again threatens nothing less than the bishop. }
8... Bh7
9. Bd3 { [%ts 3:04] By far White's most common move here, with over 20,000 games: trade off Black's light-squared bishop, which has moved several times, for one that moved once. }
9... Bxd3 { [%ts 3:12] Black doesn't really have an alternative to the trade. }
( 9... Ngf6 ) ( 9... Ndf6 )
10. Qxd3 { [%ts 3:20] White argues he has better development and more control of the center. In exchange, h4 and h5 weakened his kingside, so White can never really castle there. }
