[Event "The main lines at a glance"]
[ChapterName "The main line: provoking c3 and c4"]
[Orientation "black"]
[LeadIn "1.e4 c6 2.d4 d5 3.Nc3 dxe4 4.Nxe4 Bf5 5.Ng3 Bg6 6.h4 h6 7.Nf3 Nd7 8.h5 Bh7 9.Bd3 Bxd3 10.Qxd3"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "r2qkbnr/pp1nppp1/2p4p/7P/3P4/3Q1NN1/PPP2PP1/R1B1K2R b KQkq - 0 10"]

{ [%ts 3:37] The main line after Bxd3 Qxd3. Black now has to develop, and the main idea is ...e6, opening up the dark-squared bishop. }
10... e6
( 10... Ngf6 ) ( 10... Qc7 ) ( 10... a5 ) ( 10... Rc8 ) ( 10... Qb8 )
11. Bf4
11... Qa5+ { [%ts 4:01] Here's one of Black's ideas in the main line: force White to weaken the queenside as well. The check makes White retreat the bishop to d2 or play c3. }
( 11... Ngf6 ) ( 11... Bb4+ ) ( 11... Be7 )
12. Bd2
12... Bb4 { [%ts 4:10] Challenging the bishop once again. }
( 12... Qc7 ) ( 12... Qb6 )
13. c3 { [%ts 4:14] White answers with c3, another queenside weakening. }
13... Be7 { [%ts 4:18] The bishop simply retreats. It could have gone straight from f8 to e7, so Black lost no time, and this way it provoked c3. }
( 13... Bd6 ) ( 13... Bf8 )
14. c4 { [%ts 4:26] Now c4 attacks the queen: the bishop on d2 sees a5 again. }
14... Qc7 { [%ts 4:30] Black spent only a couple of moves and provoked c3, c4 and the bishop's retreat from f4 to d2. A favorable version for Black, even if White keeps a slight opening edge. }
( 14... Qb6 ) ( 14... Qa6 ) ( 14... Qd8 )
15. O-O-O { [%ts 4:51] White most commonly castles queenside, and the king will step to b1. Kingside castling is too risky after the h-pawn's advance. }
15... Ngf6
( 15... Rd8 ) ( 15... b5 ) ( 15... a6 ) ( 15... Rc8 )
16. Kb1
16... O-O { [%ts 5:05] Black castles: the starting position of the main line. Engines give Black about half a pawn, partly because the h5 pawn is overextended and the knight on g3 is fairly useless. Practically it's roughly level, but Black's king is safer and his queenside attack is ready-made. }
( 16... Rd8 ) ( 16... a5 ) ( 16... b5 ) ( 16... a6 ) ( 16... Ng4 ) ( 16... Rb8 )
