[Event "The main lines at a glance"]
[ChapterName "The Karpov Variation: 4...Nd7"]
[Orientation "black"]
[LeadIn "1.e4 c6 2.d4 d5 3.Nc3 dxe4 4.Nxe4"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pp2pppp/2p5/8/3PN3/8/PPP2PPP/R1BQKBNR b KQkq - 0 4"]

{ [%ts 7:27] Back to move four, after Nxe4. Bf5 was the main move, but Black has two more very good options that give even more activity. }
4... Nd7 { [%ts 7:51] The Karpov Variation. Black doesn't challenge the e4 knight yet: he prepares Ngf6 so he can recapture with the other knight, and the bishop stays on c8, where h4 and h5 can't chase it. }
( 4... Bf5 { [%ts 7:43] The main line, also good. This chapter is about the Karpov: find the knight move. } )
( 4... Nf6 ) ( 4... g6 ) ( 4... h6 ) ( 4... a5 )
5. Nf3
( 5. Ng5 { [%ts 9:47] Ng5 strikes at f7 immediately, so Black has to be a little careful. } 5... Ngf6 { [%ts 10:04] The best move, and the most commonly played. } ( 5... h6 ) ( 5... e6 ) ( 5... Qc7 ) ( 5... Ndf6 ) 6. Bd3 6... e6 { [%ts 10:12] White stands a bit better, but Black has compensation for White's lost tempi. Chasing the knight with h6 is okay, but it doesn't have to be played yet: White doesn't have many threats. } ( 6... g6 ) ( 6... Qc7 ) ( 6... e5 ) ( 6... Nb6 ) )
( 5. Qe2 { [%ts 10:28] A scary idea you have to remember: Qe2 sets up a mate. } 5... Ndf6 { [%ts 10:45] Bring the d7 knight to f6, so that if White takes, you recapture and the d-file opens for your queen. Playing e6 to give the king a square, so the bishop covers d6, also works. } ( 5... e6 { [%ts 10:53] The other safe option: the king gets a square and the bishop can capture on d6. Both work; here, play the knight move. } ) ( 5... Qc7 ) ( 5... Nb6 ) ( 5... Ngf6 $4 { [%ts 10:37] Developing normally loses at once: Nd6 is checkmate. The knight can't be taken and the king has no moves. } ) 6. Nxf6+ 6... Nxf6 { [%ts 10:45] Black recaptures on f6 and is safe. } ( 6... gxf6 ) )
5... Ngf6 { [%ts 8:25] Black continues with Ngf6, and now White is forced to capture. }
( 5... Ndf6 ) ( 5... g6 )
6. Nxf6+ { [%ts 8:25] Forced: if White doesn't take on f6, Black takes on e4. }
( 6. Bd3 { [%ts 8:41] Say White defends with Bd3 instead of taking. Black takes on e4, and after Bxe4 the d7 knight comes to f6 with tempo on the bishop. } 6... Nxe4 ( 6... Qc7 ) ( 6... g6 ) 7. Bxe4 7... Ndf6 { [%ts 8:49] Ndf6 hits the bishop on e4, and Black gains a tempo. } ( 7... g6 ) ( 7... a6 ) ( 7... Qc7 ) ( 7... Qb6 ) ( 7... a5 ) )
6... Nxf6 { [%ts 8:58] Development is equal: both sides have spent two moves and developed one piece and one pawn each. }
( 6... exf6 )
7. Bc4 { [%ts 9:06] The most active move for White. The bishop stares at f7, ready to double the attack with a knight on g5 or e5, and maybe Qe2 later. }
7... Bf5 { [%ts 9:22] Black still has to develop the light-squared bishop before playing e6 and closing the diagonal to secure f7. }
( 7... Qc7 ) ( 7... b5 ) ( 7... Nd5 ) ( 7... Qd6 ) ( 7... e6 { The engine is fine with it too, but Stjepan wants the bishop out before e6 closes its diagonal. } )
8. O-O
8... e6 { [%ts 9:30] Now e6, and this is the standard line in the Karpov Variation. }
( 8... Qc7 ) ( 8... h6 ) ( 8... a5 ) ( 8... a6 )
