[Event "The main lines at a glance"]
[ChapterName "4...Nf6: the Korchnoi Variation"]
[Orientation "black"]
[LeadIn "1.e4 c6 2.d4 d5 3.Nc3 dxe4 4.Nxe4"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pp2pppp/2p5/8/3PN3/8/PPP2PPP/R1BQKBNR b KQkq - 0 4"]

{ [%ts 11:10] One more line after Nxe4 that isn't Bf5 or the Karpov: challenge the knight with your own. }
4... Nf6 { [%ts 11:18] The second most popular continuation. It branches into two variations. }
( 4... Nd7 { [%ts 11:18] 4...Nd7, the Karpov, is also good. This chapter is about ...Nf6. } )
( 4... Bf5 { [%ts 11:18] The main line, also good. This chapter is about the knight move that challenges e4. } )
( 4... g6 ) ( 4... h6 ) ( 4... a5 )
5. Nxf6+ { [%ts 11:34] White has to capture: if he lets the knight be challenged, it just goes back with tempo. }
5... exf6 { [%ts 11:50] Taking with the e-pawn is the Korchnoi Variation. It's a very drawish line that doesn't give much counterplay. }
( 5... gxf6 $6 { [%ts 12:31] The Bronstein-Larsen. Stjepan says it gives Black a lot of initiative but White is supposed to be positionally better, and the engine rates it a clear step down from exf6. The last chapter shows how it goes. Here, take with the e-pawn. } )
6. c3 { [%ts 11:58] White sets up with c3, Bd3, a knight to f3 and kingside castling. }
6... Bd6 { [%ts 11:58] The most common plan: Black develops his bishops to d6 and e6. }
( 6... Nd7 ) ( 6... a5 ) ( 6... Qc7 ) ( 6... g6 )
7. Bd3
7... O-O { [%ts 12:06] Both sides castle kingside and the middlegame is equal, if slightly boring. The catch: any endgame is unfavorable for Black because of the doubled f-pawns, a bit like the Berlin, where Black has no kingside pawn majority. }
( 7... Nd7 ) ( 7... c5 ) ( 7... Qc7 ) ( 7... Na6 )
*

[Event "The main lines at a glance"]
[ChapterName "If you choose the Bronstein-Larsen"]
[Orientation "black"]
[LeadIn "1.e4 c6 2.d4 d5 3.Nc3 dxe4 4.Nxe4 Nf6 5.Nxf6+ gxf6"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "rnbqkb1r/pp2pp1p/2p2p2/8/3P4/8/PPP2PPP/R1BQKBNR w KQkq - 0 6"]

{ [%ts 12:31] Recapturing with the g-pawn is the Bronstein-Larsen. Stjepan warns that White is supposed to be positionally and strategically better, and the engine agrees, but Black gets a lot of initiative for the doubled pawns and the broken kingside. }
6. c3
6... Bf5 { [%ts 12:48] Black develops the bishop to f5, and both sides keep developing. }
( 6... Qd5 ) ( 6... h5 ) ( 6... e5 ) ( 6... Na6 ) ( 6... Qd6 )
7. Nf3
7... e6 { [%ts 12:56] Black has a lot of activity with the bishop pair. The plan is to castle queenside and put a rook on g8 to strike down the g-file, so White will have a hard time castling kingside. In any endgame, though, Black is slightly worse because of the ruined kingside structure. }
( 7... Nd7 ) ( 7... Bg6 ) ( 7... h5 ) ( 7... a5 )
